# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*
!.env.example

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# operator outreach data
/.outreach/
/outreach/runs/

# Local brainstorming companion sessions
/.superpowers/

# Agent tooling state, not project content.
# `.claude/skills/` is a directory of symlinks into `.agents/skills/`, so both
# are the same vendored library and neither belongs in history.
# `.agents/product-marketing.md` is deliberately NOT ignored — it is authored
# positioning copy and the launch gate cites it as evidence.
/.claude/
/.agents/skills/

# Scratch and generated output from local verification runs
/tmp/
/output/
/.playwright-cli/

# Promo video. The rule below used to keep the authored source
# (STORYBOARD.md, SCRIPT.md, DESIGN.md, compositions/, *.json) visible and
# ignore only the rendered media, on the assumption the source would be
# committed. It never was. Video work was closed by owner decision on
# 20 August 2026 and the shipped demo video was built elsewhere, in
# ~/Documents/Hackathon/video-build/, so /videos/ holds a dead July project
# whose 13 authored files sat untracked-but-not-ignored in a public
# repository — one `git add .` from being committed. The whole directory is
# now ignored. Nothing has been deleted; the files remain on disk. If video
# work ever restarts, narrow this back to the media-only rules kept below.
/videos/
# Retained so the media rules still apply if /videos/ is ever un-ignored.
/videos/**/renders/
/videos/**/capture/
/videos/**/snapshots/
/videos/**/voice-auditions/
/videos/**/.waveform-cache/
/videos/**/.thumbnails/
*.wav
*.aiff
*.mp4
*.mov

# Google Cloud service-account key files. Nothing here expects one to exist:
# credentials are supplied as environment variables (GCS_CLIENT_EMAIL,
# GCS_PRIVATE_KEY — see .env.example), so a key file on disk is always
# either a mistake or a temporary debugging artefact. The rules below exist
# because the repository is PUBLIC, and a committed service-account key is
# an immediate compromise plus a forced rotation, not an embarrassment.
# This is the same failure shape as the /videos/ directory above: files that
# were untracked but not ignored, one `git add .` from being committed.
# Added 1 September 2026 after an audit found no key material committed
# anywhere, but no rule preventing it either.
*service-account*.json
*service_account*.json
gcp-*.json
gcloud-*.json
credentials.json
client_secret*.json
brightcert-sa*.json
*-key.json
